WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:04.410
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:09.910
OpenAI GPT-2 is a learning-based technique
which can perform common natural language

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:17.380
processing operations, for instance, answering
questions, completing text, reading comprehension,

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:19.669
summarization, and more.

00:00:19.669 --> 00:00:24.759
What is absolutely amazing about this technique
is that it is able to perform these tasks

00:00:24.759 --> 00:00:27.669
with as little supervision as possible.

00:00:27.669 --> 00:00:32.970
This means that we unleash the algorithm that
they call GPT-2 to read the internet, and

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:35.170
it will learn our language by itself.

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:41.860
To perform this, of course, we need a lot
of training data, and here, the AI reads 40

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:48.399
gigabytes of internet text, which is 40 gigs
of non-binary plaintext data, which is a stupendously

00:00:48.399 --> 00:00:49.879
large amount of text.

00:00:49.879 --> 00:00:55.760
You see here an example of how it fares with
text completion, and the results were absolutely

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:56.820
amazing.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:03.120
And, not only that, because, hold on to your
papers, we can now try it ourselves and my

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:09.700
results show that it can be wise as a scholar,
and it can also cheat like nobody’s business.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:12.270
So, let’s try something simple here.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:17.090
Well, first, as everyone is talking about
job security, I’d like to give it the start

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:21.490
of this Two Minute Papers script and see if
I am about to be replaced.

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:22.810
Let’s see!

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:29.710
It continues the intro with “The history
of Eastern Europe since 1989 has been an interesting

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:33.170
and complex story spanning four decades.

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:39.560
On one side you have a regime of Western-backed
dictators from the mid-1990s” and so on.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.170
Very scholarly indeed.

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:45.170
However, I think I might be safe …for now.

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:50.810
And now, something that I wanted to try since
the inception of GPT-2, which is, feed it

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:56.050
a snippet of something that I said about Wavelet
Turbulence, which is one of the best papers

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:59.220
ever written, and have the AI finish it.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:04.480
I tried to frame it as an exam question and
was not disappointed be the results.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:10.509
So the text is, “There was a similar technique
by the name Wavelet Turbulence, which is”,

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:14.520
I hope you see what I did there…and look
what it did!

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:19.280
“There was a similar technique by the name
Wavelet Turbulence, which is not at all what

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:24.530
we are talking about, but I'll let you decide
whether it is worth your time to read it.”

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:25.530
Wow!

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:28.880
GPT-2 has been caught cheating red handed.

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.370
I love it.

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:35.810
Let’s continue what it had to say: “Anyway,
my aim here is to describe how we use a fluid

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:42.670
simulation to simulate smoke and liquid effects
using just a few simple equations: Solving

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:48.710
a few functions gives us a set of derivatives
of the particle trajectories, that is equations

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:54.180
that describe how and where the particles
will come and go across the liquid in the

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:55.180
simulation.

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:59.950
For simplicity (as a good teacher of high-school
students should know) we can use a simple

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:03.920
equation, which simulates two particles that
collide.”

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:05.550
I am stunned.

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:10.420
It recognized that we are talking about fluid
simulations, which is already remarkable,

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:16.440
but it went much further - The completion
is not bad at all and is not only coherent,

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.440
on topic, but has quite a bit of truth to
it.

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:23.820
I will have to rethink my previous claim about
my job security.

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:29.880
The even crazier thing is that the size of
this model is about 750 million parameters,

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:35.740
which is only half of the size of the original
full model, which is expected to be even better.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:40.400
I put a link to this website in the video
description for your pleasure, make sure to

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:43.330
play with it, this is mad fun.

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:49.040
And, GPT-2 will also see so many applications
that we cannot even fathom yet.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:54.300
For instance, here you can see that one can
train it on many source code files on GitHub

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:58.610
and it will be able to complete the code that
we write on the fly.

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:04.850
Now, nobody should think of this as GPT-2
writing programs for us, this is, of course,

00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:10.220
unlikely, however, it will ease the process
for novice and experts users alike.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:14.700
If you have any other novel applications in
mind, make sure to leave a comment below.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:20.969
For now, bravo OpenAI, and a big thank you
for Daniel King and the Hugging Face company

00:04:20.969 --> 00:04:24.169
for this super convenient public implementation.

00:04:24.169 --> 00:04:36.990
Let the 
experiments begin!

00:04:36.990 --> 00:05:21.529
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

